Relevant columns and their possible roles  

MNPR01_REFERENCE_201703  
• "name": reference‐sequence identifier – will be used to join to the variant tables.  
• "length": size (bp) of the reference sequence – required for the denominator when computing “variant density”.  

MNPR01_201703  
• "reference_name": identifier matching the reference “name”.  
• "start": genomic coordinate – every distinct coordinate that survives the filters is one variant event.  
• "call": VARIANT column that stores an array of per–sample call objects; needs to be flattened and inspected.  
  Inside each call object → "genotype" (array) – a variant is considered present if at least one element of this array is > 0.  

Typical conditions that can be used  
1. Join condition:
   r."name" = v."reference_name"  
2. Variant present:
   EXISTS( SELECT 1 FROM LATERAL FLATTEN(input => v."call") c  
           WHERE ARRAY_MAX( c.value:"genotype" )::INT > 0 )  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL statements (each returns ≤ 20 rows) that progress from simple inspection to building the final “variant density” result.  
All identifiers are wrapped in double quotes and every query is preceded by a short comment.

```sql
-- 1. Description: show the 20 longest reference sequences
SELECT 
    "name",
    "length"
FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_REFERENCE_201703"
ORDER BY "length" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: list distinct reference names that actually contain at least one variant call
SELECT DISTINCT 
    "reference_name"
FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_201703"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: sample the raw structure of the "call" column
SELECT 
    "reference_name",
    "start",
    "call"
FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_201703"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: show one flattened call record to inspect inner keys (genotype, AD, etc.)
SELECT 
    v."reference_name",
    v."start",
    c.value AS "one_call_object"
FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_201703" v,
     LATERAL FLATTEN( input => v."call" ) c
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: isolate calls where any genotype allele is > 0 (i.e., a non‑reference genotype)
SELECT 
    v."reference_name",
    v."start",
    c.value:"genotype" AS "genotype_array"
FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_201703" v,
     LATERAL FLATTEN( input => v."call" ) c
WHERE ARRAY_MAX( c.value:"genotype" )::INT > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: count variants (with ≥1 non‑reference genotype) per reference
SELECT 
    v."reference_name",
    COUNT(DISTINCT v."start") AS "variant_count"
FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_201703" v,
     LATERAL FLATTEN( input => v."call" ) c
WHERE ARRAY_MAX( c.value:"genotype" )::INT > 0
GROUP BY v."reference_name"
ORDER BY "variant_count" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: obtain the single longest reference (name and length)
SELECT 
    "name"  AS "longest_ref_name",
    "length"
FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_REFERENCE_201703"
ORDER BY "length" DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: count variants on the longest reference only
WITH longest AS (
    SELECT "name" 
    FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_REFERENCE_201703"
    ORDER BY "length" DESC
    LIMIT 1
)
SELECT 
    v."reference_name",
    COUNT(DISTINCT v."start") AS "variant_count"
FROM longest l
JOIN GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_201703" v
      ON v."reference_name" = l."name",
     LATERAL FLATTEN( input => v."call" ) c
WHERE ARRAY_MAX( c.value:"genotype" )::INT > 0
GROUP BY v."reference_name"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: join variant counts with reference lengths to compute variant density for all refs
WITH var_ct AS (
    SELECT 
        v."reference_name",
        COUNT(DISTINCT v."start") AS "variant_count"
    FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_201703" v,
         LATERAL FLATTEN( input => v."call" ) c
    WHERE ARRAY_MAX( c.value:"genotype" )::INT > 0
    GROUP BY v."reference_name"
)
SELECT 
    r."name",
    r."length",
    vc."variant_count",
    vc."variant_count" / r."length" AS "variant_density"
FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_REFERENCE_201703" r
LEFT JOIN var_ct vc ON r."name" = vc."reference_name"
ORDER BY "variant_density" DESC NULLS LAST
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: final density calculation for the single longest reference
WITH longest AS (
    SELECT "name", "length"
    FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_REFERENCE_201703"
    ORDER BY "length" DESC
    LIMIT 1
),
var_ct AS (
    SELECT 
        v."reference_name",
        COUNT(DISTINCT v."start") AS "variant_count"
    FROM GENOMICS_CANNABIS.GENOMICS_CANNABIS."MNPR01_201703" v
    JOIN longest l ON v."reference_name" = l."name",
         LATERAL FLATTEN( input => v."call" ) c
    WHERE ARRAY_MAX( c.value:"genotype" )::INT > 0
    GROUP BY v."reference_name"
)
SELECT 
    l."name"                                       AS "reference_name",
    l."length",
    vc."variant_count",
    vc."variant_count" / l."length"                AS "variant_density"
FROM longest l
LEFT JOIN var_ct vc ON l."name" = vc."reference_name"
LIMIT 20;
```

The above queries should let you step through the data, inspect the nested “call” structure, verify the genotype test, and finally compute the requested density for the longest cannabis reference sequence.
